---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const article = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'UAE Visa from Pakistan — All Types Explained',
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'VisaPath.pk' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'VisaPath.pk' },
  datePublished: '2026-10-24',
};

const faq = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Can Pakistanis get UAE visa on arrival?',
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'No. Pakistani passport holders need a pre-arranged visa before traveling to the UAE. You cannot get visa on arrival.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'How long is UAE tourist visa valid?',
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Standard UAE tourist visa is valid for 30 or 60 days from entry. Long-term options include 5-year multiple entry visa and 10-year Golden Visa for eligible applicants.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'What is UAE Golden Visa?',
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'UAE Golden Visa is a 10-year renewable residence permit for investors, entrepreneurs, scientists, professionals, outstanding students, and others meeting specific criteria.' } },
  ],
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="UAE Visa from Pakistan — All Types Explained 2026"
  description="Complete guide to UAE visa types for Pakistanis: 30/60-day tourist, 5-year multiple entry, Golden Visa, employment visa, visit visa."
  schema={[article, faq]}
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <nav class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-brand-600">Home</a> ›
      <a href="/guides/" class="hover:text-brand-600">Guides</a> ›
      <span>UAE Visa Types</span>
    </nav>

    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-4">UAE Visa from Pakistan — All Types Explained</h1>
    <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm mb-8">Last updated: October 2026 · 9 minute read</p>

    <section class="prose max-w-none">
      <p class="lead text-lg">
        The UAE is one of the most-visited destinations for Pakistani travelers — for tourism, business, employment, Umrah transits, and family visits. This guide explains every visa type available to Pakistani passport holders in 2026.
      </p>

      <h2>Pakistanis Do Not Get Visa on Arrival</h2>
      <p>
        Despite the UAE's reputation for easy tourism, Pakistani passport holders must have a visa arranged <strong>before</strong> arrival. Arriving without one results in denied boarding at the Pakistani airport.
      </p>

      <h2>Tourist Visa (Short Stay)</h2>
      <h3>30-Day Single Entry</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Allows one entry, stay up to 30 days</li>
        <li>Can be extended once for 30 more days (fees apply)</li>
        <li>Fee: approximately $90 USD</li>
        <li>Processing: 3–5 working days</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>60-Day Single Entry</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Longer tourist visa for extended visits</li>
        <li>Fee: approximately $180 USD</li>
        <li>Suitable for family visits or extended holidays</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Multiple Entry (90-Day Validity)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Allows multiple entries within 90 days</li>
        <li>Each stay up to 60 days</li>
        <li>Good for business travelers with multiple trips</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>5-Year Multiple Entry Tourist Visa</h3>
      <p>
        Introduced for better connectivity. Allows multiple visits over 5 years, each stay up to 90 days (extendable by another 90 days once). Fee approximately $190 USD. Available to Pakistani applicants meeting financial criteria.
      </p>

      <h2>How to Apply</h2>
      <h3>Through an Airline</h3>
      <p>
        Emirates, Etihad, flydubai, and Air Arabia all offer visa sponsorship when you book a return ticket on their airline. Fastest and most common route for Pakistanis.
      </p>

      <h3>Through a Hotel</h3>
      <p>
        Many UAE hotels sponsor tourist visas when you book a stay with them. Convenient package deals through platforms like Dnata, Al Khoory, Holiday Factory.
      </p>

      <h3>Through UAE-Based Sponsor</h3>
      <p>
        If you have a UAE resident family member or friend (with Emirates ID), they can sponsor your visit visa through ICP or GDRFA portals.
      </p>

      <h3>Direct Online Application</h3>
      <p>
        Via smartservices.icp.gov.ae (Federal Authority for Identity, Citizenship, Customs & Port Security). Requires sponsor information or visa agent registration.
      </p>

      <h2>Documents Needed</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Passport scan (color, valid 6+ months)</li>
        <li>Recent photograph (white background, passport style)</li>
        <li>Confirmed return flight ticket</li>
        <li>Hotel booking confirmation or sponsor's UAE address</li>
        <li>Bank statement showing financial capacity</li>
        <li>Employment letter or business documents</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Visit Visa (Family-Sponsored)</h2>
      <p>
        A UAE resident (family member or friend) with Emirates ID can sponsor your visit:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>30 days or 90 days validity</li>
        <li>Sponsor must earn minimum salary (varies by relationship)</li>
        <li>Sponsor applies via ICP smart services or GDRFA Dubai</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Transit Visa</h2>
      <p>
        If connecting through UAE to another destination with over 8 hours between flights, you can get:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>48-hour transit visa:</strong> free (airline-arranged)</li>
        <li><strong>96-hour transit visa:</strong> small fee, allows city visit</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Employment Visa</h2>
      <p>
        To work in the UAE, you need an employer-sponsored employment visa. Process:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>UAE employer applies for your Work Permit via MOHRE</li>
        <li>Pink entry permit issued — you enter UAE on this</li>
        <li>Medical fitness test in UAE</li>
        <li>Emirates ID application</li>
        <li>Residence visa stamping in passport</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Valid for 2 years, renewable. Your employer handles most of the process. You attest documents (education certificates) in Pakistan before travel.
      </p>

      <h2>Golden Visa (10 Years)</h2>
      <p>
        The UAE Golden Visa is a 10-year renewable residence for exceptional categories:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Investors:</strong> property worth AED 2M+ or capital of AED 2M+</li>
        <li><strong>Entrepreneurs:</strong> project owners valued at AED 500K+</li>
        <li><strong>Specialised talents:</strong> doctors, scientists, inventors, academics</li>
        <li><strong>Outstanding students:</strong> top-ranking high school or university graduates</li>
        <li><strong>Humanitarian pioneers</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Highest income earners:</strong> monthly salary AED 30,000+ in specific sectors</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Golden Visa benefits:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>No employer sponsor needed</li>
        <li>Family inclusion (spouse, children, parents)</li>
        <li>Multiple-entry, no "6-month rule" for absence</li>
        <li>Right to work in any UAE company</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Student Visa</h2>
      <p>
        For enrollment at a UAE university or school. Sponsored by the educational institution. Typical duration: 1 year, renewable annually until course completion.
      </p>

      <h2>Family Residence Visa</h2>
      <p>
        UAE residents (employees or Golden Visa holders) can sponsor spouse, children, and parents for residence. Minimum salary requirements:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>To sponsor family: typically AED 4,000+ monthly salary with housing, or AED 5,000+ without</li>
        <li>Marriage certificate attested from Pakistan Foreign Office</li>
        <li>Children's birth certificates attested</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Dubai vs Abu Dhabi vs Other Emirates</h2>
      <p>
        UAE visas issued in one Emirate are valid for the entire country. However:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Dubai visas processed via GDRFA Dubai</li>
        <li>Abu Dhabi and other Emirates via ICP (Federal Authority)</li>
        <li>Processing times and specific requirements vary slightly</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>After Arrival</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>No separate entry stamp at airport — your e-visa is scanned on arrival</li>
        <li>Keep digital and printed copy during stay</li>
        <li>Overstay fines: AED 50 per day of overstay</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Related Resources</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="/tools/passport-validity-checker/">Passport Validity Checker</a> ·
        <a href="/tools/visa-fee-calculator/">Fee Calculator</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
